    /// Installs a charging limit.
    ///
    /// Sends the request to the charging station and blocks the HTTP request
    /// until it answers or ~35s elapses. The station's own accept/reject
    /// decision is never reported as an HTTP error: `201` means it accepted
    /// the limit, which now exists as an addressable resource (`limit` names
    /// it, and `Location` points at it); `200` means the station answered but
    /// rejected it (`accepted: false`, `limit` absent), so nothing was
    /// created. The codes below are for cases the station never got to decide
    /// at all: `409` means it was not connected; `422` means the request
    /// itself would exceed what this station has told the CSMS it can hold,
    /// or the schedule's `recurrence`/`anchor` combination is
    /// self-contradictory; `502` means the station disconnected mid-flight or
    /// answered with something this service could not interpret; `504` means
    /// nothing came back before the deadline; `503` means the request could
